# Review standard for the delivery (internal-review-repo)

Source: the `Research objective` issue (internal-review-repo#1). The brief (`idea-repo`) and the
work assignment (`story-repo`) were not yet published when this was written; the standard will be
re-checked against them and against the venue requirements from `venue-repo` once they exist.
Every item below is checked on the assembled delivery (`ht-role delivery`) at the exact revisions
it is assembled from. I re-run the code and re-compile the LaTeX myself; I do not take numbers or
figures on trust.

## R1 Data
- Iris, OpenML dataset id 61, downloaded from OpenML by the delivered code (not sklearn's bundled
  copy, not a hand-made CSV). The download URL/API call, dataset version and a checksum of the file
  used are recorded. 150 instances, 4 numeric features, 3 classes of 50 each is verified in code
  output.

## R2 Methods
- Multinomial logistic regression: genuinely multinomial (softmax) loss, not one-vs-rest; solver,
  regularisation (C), iterations and any feature scaling stated. Convergence warnings, if any, are
  addressed.
- k-NN with k chosen by inner cross-validation: nested inside each outer training fold only
  (no tuning on outer test data); the k grid, inner CV scheme and tie rule are stated; the k chosen
  in each outer fold is recorded and summarised. Any scaling sits in a pipeline fitted on training
  data only (same rule for LR).

## R3 Evaluation design
- 10 repetitions x stratified 5-fold = 50 outer folds, the same splits for both methods (paired),
  seeds fixed and recorded. Per-fold accuracies for both methods are saved to a results file.

## R4 Uncertainty and claims
- Each method: mean accuracy with a 95% interval; paired difference (with sign convention stated)
  with a 95% interval. The interval method is named and justified. Folds of repeated CV share
  training data, so a naive t-interval over 50 folds treated as independent understates the
  uncertainty; the report must use a method that accounts for this (e.g. the Nadeau-Bengio
  corrected resampled t, or an interval over the 10 repetition means) or state clearly why its
  choice is adequate and what it ignores. Ties/discreteness of accuracy on 30-sample test folds
  are handled sensibly (e.g. zero-variance difference cases).
- Every claim in the text is supported by the interval it cites; no conclusion is required in
  advance (a difference that is not distinguishable from zero must be reported as such).

## R5 Ibex vs local
- The full cross-validation is run once as a CPU job on Ibex (SLURM) and once locally, with the same
  code. Evidence delivered: job id, the SLURM output/log, the node name as reported by the job
  itself (e.g. `$SLURMD_NODENAME`/`hostname`) or by `sacct`, software versions on both sides, and the
  per-fold result files of both runs.
- The claim "identical" or "not identical" is backed by a comparison of the full per-fold outputs
  (exact equality or hash), not just rounded summaries; any difference is quantified and explained.

## R6 Paper
- `report/report.pdf`, at most two pages, built with the official NeurIPS 2026 style file in
  submission (anonymous) mode, compiled from the delivered LaTeX source (I re-compile it and compare).
  Page counting follows `venue-repo`'s stated interpretation; absent one, the whole PDF must fit in
  two pages.
- Exactly one figure and one table. The figure is opened and inspected: not blank or placeholder,
  axes labelled with units, legible at print size, and showing what the text says it shows. The
  table matches the results files to the printed precision.
- The LaTeX source and the code that produced every number sit next to the PDF (under `report/`
  or paths the assignment declares), with instructions to run them. Every number in the PDF traces
  to a code output; I re-run the code locally and check the numbers reproduce.
- The report states the method, the evaluation design, the interval methods, the Ibex node name
  and the identical/not-identical result, and the limitations (small data, single dataset).

## Verdict
- Approve only when every item holds on the inspected revision. Findings go as `revise` decisions to
  the owning position (analysis -> experiment-repo, figure/table -> figtab-repo via a neighbour,
  report and layout -> paper-repo, format -> venue-repo).
